messages.thinking:
**Unearthing a Hidden Efficiency**

Okay, hold on a minute... I was just poking around and stumbled onto something interesting. First, I noticed `app/assets/images/boost.svg` and, more importantly, line 49 in `app/assets/stylesheets/icons.css`: `.icon--boost { --svg: url("boost.svg "); }`.  That's a direct connection to `icon_tag "boost"`!  It was already in the asset pipeline, primed and ready to go. I wonder where it's being *used*??

Then I shifted my attention to `app/views/cards/display/preview/_comments.html.erb`. I paired that with `app/assets/stylesheets/card-columns.css` (lines 541-553), and `app/views/cards/display/_preview.html.erb` for context. The goal is to see how the comments are *rendered*.

In `app/views/cards/display/_preview.html.erb` I see a `footer` that renders `meta`, `comments`, and a `background`. The `comments` are rendered as follows:

And the relevant CSS for the comment rendering is here in `app/assets/stylesheets/card-columns.css`:

  .card__comments {
    --column-gap: 0.8ch;

    display: flex;
    margin-block-end: calc(var(--block-space) * -1.7);
    margin-inline-end: calc(var(--card-padding-inline) * -0.5);

    .icon {
      --icon-size: 1.6em;

      color: var(--card-color);
    }

Okay, so the `<footer class="card__footer flex gap-half">` is the crucial container. Let's break down the CSS:

1.  The `footer` is a horizontal flex container (`display: flex; column-gap: calc(var(--inline-space) / 2);`).
2.  The first child is `<div class="card__meta">` which has `inline-size: fit-content;` on desktop and `inline-size: 100%;` on mobile (`max-width: 639px`).
3.  The next child is `<div class="card__comments align-center gap-half flex-item-justify-end flex-item-no-shrink">`. Now the `flex-item-justify-end` class stands out, so I'll check what that does in `utilities.css`.

